<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout>
  <title>Section 2.3: The Sine and Cosine Functions</title>
  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

  
<sidebyside widths="20% 60%" >
<image  >
      <shortdescription>
        A unit circle showing the sine function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-3-sine">
        <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="20">
          <definition>
            f(t)=(cos(t*pi/12),sin(t*pi/12))
          </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.2,-1.2,1.2,1.2)">
            <axes decorations="no"/>
            <circle center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="blue"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),f(3.6))" stroke="red"/>
            <label anchor="1/2*f(3.6)" alignment="nw" ><m>1</m></label>
            <angle-marker points="(f(0),(0,0),f(3.6))" offset="(0,-5)">
              <m>t</m>
            </angle-marker>  
            <point p="f(3.6)" alignment="e"><m>(a,b)</m></point>     
            <label anchor="(.8,1.2)"><m>\sin(t)=b</m></label>   
            <label anchor="(.8,1.05)"><m>\cos(t)=a</m></label> 


          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  <p>
    Have your unit circles out!  Use them as a reference.  We are going to turn each coordinate 
    of the points on the unit circle into a new function.
  </p>
     </sidebyside>

  <definition>
    <title>Definition 2.3.4</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Given a central angle in the unit circle with angle <m>t</m>
 radians and the terminal point <m>(a,b)</m>,
 as shown in the graph, we define the <term>sine of <m>t</m></term>, denoted <m>\sin(t)</m>,
 by the rule <md>\sin(t) = b.</md>
 That is, the <m>y</m>-coordinate on the unit circle IS the <m>\sin(t)</m>!</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>



  <definition>
    <title>Definition 2.3.8.</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Given a central angle in the unit circle that measures <m>t</m>
 radians and the terminal point <m>(a,b)</m>,
 as shown in the graph, we define the <term>cosine of <m>t</m></term>, denoted <m>\cos(t)</m>,
 by the rule <md>\cos(t) = a.</md>
 That is, the <m>x</m>-coordinate on the unit circle IS the <m>\cos(t)</m>!</p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

    


  <p>We'll start with the sine function.  A table of values and a graph of the function <m>\sin(t)</m> are below.</p>
  
<tabular>
  <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>t</cell>
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{2\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
   
  </row>
  <row  bottom="major">
    <cell><m>\sin(t)</m></cell>
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
    
  </row>
  
   <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>t</cell>
    
    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{7\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{4\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{7\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{11\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
  </row>


  <row>
    <cell><m>\sin(t)</m></cell>
    
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt2}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>-\frac{\sqrt1}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
  </row>
</tabular>


 <image width="85%">
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of the sine function.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-3-b">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>
            f(t)=sin(t)
          </definition>
          <definition>points=['0','pi/6']</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi/4,-1.25,2*pi+pi/4,1.25)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" h-pi-format="yes" 
            spacings="((-pi/4,pi/4,9*pi/4), (-1.5,.25,1.5))" 
            hlabels = "(0,pi/4,2*pi)" xlabel="t"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(4.9,1.1)"><m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m></label>  
            <point p="(0,f(0))"/>
            <point p="(pi/6,f(pi/6))"/>
            <point p="(pi/4,f(pi/4))"/>
            <point p="(pi/3,f(pi/3))"/>
            <point p="(pi/2,f(pi/2))"/>
            <point p="(2*pi/3,f(2*pi/3))"/>
            <point p="(3*pi/4,f(3*pi/4))"/>
            <point p="(5*pi/6,f(5*pi/6))" alignment="e"><m>(\frac{5\pi}{6},\frac12)</m></point>
            <point p="(pi,f(pi))"/>
            <point p="(7*pi/6,f(7*pi/6))"/>
            <point p="(5*pi/4,f(5*pi/4))"/>
            <point p="(4*pi/3,f(4*pi/3))"/>
            <point p="(3*pi/2,f(3*pi/2))"/>
            <point p="(5*pi/3,f(5*pi/3))"/>
            <point p="(7*pi/4,f(7*pi/4))"/>
            <point p="(11*pi/6,f(11*pi/6))"/>
            <point p="(2*pi,f(2*pi))"/>

          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.3.2. The cosine function</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let <m>k=g(t)</m>
 be the function that tracks the <m>x</m>-coordinate of a point traversing the unit circle counterclockwise from <m>(1,0)</m>.
 That is, <m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m>.
 Use the information we know about the unit circle to respond to the following questions.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the exact value of 
 <m>\cos(\frac{\pi}{6})</m>?
 of <m>\cos(\frac{5\pi}{6})</m>
? <m>\cos(-\frac{\pi}{3})</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task >
      <statement>
        <p>Complete the following table with the exact values of <m>k=g(t) = \cos(t)</m>
 that correspond to the stated inputs <m>t</m>.</p>
        <tabular>
          <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>t</cell>
    <cell><m>0</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{2\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
   
  </row>
  <row  bottom="major">
    <cell><m>\cos(t)</m></cell>
    <cell><m>1</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt3}2</m></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    
  </row>
  
   <row bottom="minor">
    <cell>t</cell>
    
    <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{7\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{4\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{5\pi}{3}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{7\pi}{4}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>\frac{11\pi}{6}</m></cell>
    <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
  </row>


  <row>
    <cell><m>\cos(t)</m></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
    <cell></cell>
  </row>
</tabular>
        
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task >
      <statement>
        <p>On the axes provided below, sketch an accurate graph of <m>{k=g(t) = \cos(t)}</m>.
 Label the exact location of several key points on the curve.</p>
         <image width="85%">
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of the cosine function for you to fill in.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-3-cosine">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>
            f(t)=cos(t)
          </definition>
          <definition>points=['0','pi/6']</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi/4,-1.25,2*pi+pi/4,1.25)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" h-pi-format="yes" 
            spacings="((-pi/4,pi/4,9*pi/4), (-1.5,.25,1.5))" 
            hlabels = "(0,pi/4,2*pi)" xlabel="t"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(2.9,1.1)"><m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m></label>  
        
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>What is the exact value of 
 <m>\cos(\frac{11\pi}{4})</m>?
 of <m>\cos(\frac{14\pi}{3})</m>
?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.6in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give four different values of <m>t</m>
 for which <m>\cos(t) = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.6in">
      <statement>
        <p>How is the graph of <m>\cos(t)</m>
 different from the graph of <m>\sin(t)</m>?
 How are the graphs similar?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p>Recall that the sine and cosine functions are just tracking the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>-coordinates as we move around the unit circle. See <url href="https://gvsu.edu/s/0xe">gvsu.edu/s/0xe</url>
and click on sine or cosine for an animation.</p>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Properties of the sine and cosine functions</title>
    <p>For both <m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m> and <m>g(t) =\cos(t)</m>:</p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>the domain is all real numbers</p></li>
        <li><p>the range is <m>[-1,1]</m></p></li>
        <li><p>the midline is <m>y=0</m></p></li>
        <li><p>the amplitude is <m>a=1</m></p></li>
        <li><p>the period is <m>p=2\pi</m></p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage >
    <title>The Fundamental Trigonometric Identity</title>
    <p>For any real number <m>t</m>, <md>\sin^2(t) + \cos^2(t) = 1.</md></p>
  </assemblage>

  <p workspace="1in">
     
  </p>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.3.3</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Use the graph below that shows both <m>\sin(t)</m> and <m>\cos(t)</m> on the same plot to help answer the following questions.</p>
       <image width = "85%">
      <shortdescription>
        A graph of the sine and cosine functions.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-3-both">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,200)" margins="5">
          <definition>
            f(t)=sin(t)
          </definition>
          <definition>
            g(t)=cos(t)
            </definition>
          <definition>points=['0','pi/6']</definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-pi/4,-1.25,2*pi+pi/4,1.25)">
            <grid-axes decorations="yes" h-pi-format="yes" 
            spacings="((-pi/4,pi/4,9*pi/4), (-1.5,.25,1.5))" 
            hlabels = "(0,pi/4,2*pi)" xlabel="t"/>
            <graph function="f"/>
            <label anchor="(2.9,1.1)"><m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m></label>  
            <graph function="g" dash="4 4" stroke="red"/>
            <label anchor="(1.9,-1.1)"><m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m></label> 
            <vector tail="(1.3,g(1.3))" v="(pi/2,0)"/>
        
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the largest interval you can find on which <m>\sin(t)</m>
 is decreasing.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the largest interval you can find on which <m>\sin(t)</m>
 is decreasing and concave down.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the largest interval you can find on which <m>\cos(t)</m>
 is increasing.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Give an example of the largest interval you can find on which <m>\cos(t)</m>
 is increasing and concave up.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Without doing any computation, on which interval is the average rate of change of <m>g(t)=\cos(t)</m>
 greater: <m>[\pi,\pi+0.01]</m>
 or <m>[\frac{3\pi}{2},\frac{3\pi}{2}+0.01]</m>?
 Why?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>In general, how would you characterize the locations on the sine and cosine graphs where the functions are increasing or decreasingly most rapidly?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Thinking from the perspective of the unit circle, for which quadrants of the <m>x</m>-<m>y</m>
 plane is <m>\cos(t)</m>
 negative for an angle <m>t</m> 
 that lies in that quadrant?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.2.4 Going between</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>Note that almost all computing devices can evaluate <m>\sin(t)</m> and <m>\cos(t)</m> for different values of <m>t</m>, but we need to be <term>careful to tell it whether we are in degrees or radians!</term>  In desmos, the option to choose is within the wrench at the top right of the page.  Being aware of that, find the exact values whenever possible, otherwise round to at least 4 decimal places below.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The <m>x</m>-coordinate of the point on the unit circle that lies in the third quadrant and whose 
<m>y</m>-coordinate is 
<m>y=-\frac34</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The <m>y</m>-coordinate of the point on the unit circle generated by a central angle opening counterclockwise with one side on the positive 
<m>x</m>-axis that measures 
<m>t=2</m> radians.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The 
<m>x</m>-coordinate of the point on the unit circle generated by a central angle with one side on the positive 
<m>x</m>-axis that measures 
<m>t=-3.05</m> radians. (With the negative radian measure, we view the angle as opening clockwise from its initial side on the positive 
<m>x</m>-axis.)</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The value of <m>\cos(t)</m>
 where <m>t</m>
 is an angle in Quadrant II that satisfies 
 <m>\sin(t)=\frac12</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The value of <m>\sin(t)</m>
 where <m>t</m>
 is an angle in Quadrant III for which <m>\cos(t) = -0.7</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The average rate of change of <m>f(t) = \sin(t)</m>
 on the intervals <m>[0.1,0.2]</m>
 and <m>[0.8,0.9]</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>The average rate of change of <m>g(t) = \cos(t)</m> 
 on the intervals <m>[0.1,0.2]</m>
 and <m>[0.8,0.9]</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

</handout>
